WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.400
You know, like highly automated musical,

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.320
generative AI, which kind of defeats the purpose.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.480
So there's either the,

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.480
what we would call auto regressive neural codec models,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.840
or there's this idea of diffusion models.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.640
A generative model for lullabies

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.560
and the guy trained it on, like short melodic

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.680
lullabies for children,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.400
and the guy trained it on his like five or six year old

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.920
daughter.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.880
Hi, we're in Warsaw at PyWaw at the Venture café,

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.720
and I just caught Mateusz.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.400
Mateusz is a professional musician,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:41.840
software engineer, researcher,

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.960
and assistant professor at the Warsaw University of Technology.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.400
Hi, everybody.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.080
Mateusz, what are your thoughts about AI and music?

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.200
Yeah, well, that's the big question, right?

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.200
So I have some conflicting thoughts and views on the topic,

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.880
so I've been a professional musician

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.560
for a lot of years,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.280
so I know what's the feeling of being on stage

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.880
and practicing on your instrument,

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:11.040
and putting your heart into music, into performance, et cetera.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:18.480
But then also, again, a part of my heart is at research,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.560
at machine learning, at programming, et cetera.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.760
So I think in general, my whole view of this whole hot topic

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:33.920
that is music AI is that I really like,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:39.520
and I'm really interested in the parts that really empower

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.080
new interfaces for musical expression,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.960
and really empower the artists.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:52.960
And I'm not really so much like for the highly automated

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.120
musical generative AI,

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:00.880
which defeats the purpose,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.600
it's very hard for me to find space

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.960
where that would be really interesting, I think.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.840
So you said that AI has some good and bad sides,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.160
so what are the good sides of it?

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.800
Well, in modern music AI,

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.480
what do you use the most is neural networks.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.680
And neural networks are just these fascinating pieces

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.920
of maths and programming that are kind of super flexible

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.960
to learn complex data transformations,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.760
like as long as you're able to express what you're trying

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.600
to learn with code and maths,

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.680
and you have the data to support it,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.280
you're basically in many cases able to build a tool

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.840
for that transformation, right?

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.800
Oftentimes better than a human would do it, right?

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.600
So if you kind of think about neural networks

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.320
as these creative machines that you can use

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.640
for audio style transfer,

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.680
or that kind of allow you to explore or modify audio

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.760
in ways that were previously unavailable,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.360
I think that's great.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.480
I think that there are also many applications in learning music,

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.720
learning to play music, so automated software

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.280
for vocal analysis or for tracking your piano progress

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.120
or whatever.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.760
So I think the empowering side and the new interfaces side

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.560
is really, really compelling.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.560
And then on the other side,

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.600
like obviously there's this case for copyright.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.120
So again, as a performing artist and musician,

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.560
I know how hard it is to make a living as a musician.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:00.640
So it's getting even harder when streams of revenue,

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.400
such as royalties from your music getting played,

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.120
et cetera, when that's being compromised

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.640
or that model seems not to work the way it works,

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.600
right, or there are these legal and ethical disputes

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.240
that kind of leave you wondering.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:28.400
So I think as long as we think about neural networks

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.360
as this kind of next step in the evolution

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:36.640
of musical instruments, I think we're in for a lot

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.600
of super interesting inventions and ways of expressions.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.440
And how do musicians feel about it?

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.000
Do they do you think that they feel threatened

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.800
or do you think that they also see opportunities?

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.440
- Yeah, well, well,

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.560
a big part of musicians, they think that the researchers

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.240
are the bad guys, right?

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.440
Cause the algorithms are here to,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.120
to replace blah, blah, blah, blah,

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.560
it's all that, but musicians are also like super tech savvy people.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:18.400
And there's like so many musicians among programmers

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.360
like I'm sure that people who are listening to us right now,

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.240
like many of them play some sort of instrument, right?

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.880
And musicians are gear heads, they love gear,

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.160
they can spend hours talking to you know about guitars

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.680
and strings and pedals and effects and drum bass

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.960
and drum sticks and symbols and whatever.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.320
And you can argue you know for hours about that.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.600
So a part of this new technology is just basically

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:53.120
like new cool gear that you can create like cool new sounds

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.760
with and it just you know, it opens up this kind of new

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.560
dimension of tinkering and kind of tweaking algorithm.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:07.200
Sometimes it also,

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.280
it kind of requires you to have a new skill set.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.280
For instance like running Python scripts or perhaps you know,

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.600
like running the training of some model or whatever,

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.720
like it's hard to expect, you know,

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:27.280
someone to like learn Python and full just to just to explore

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:30.160
some creative possibilities, but many of these solutions,

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.480
you just need to run some sort of script, right?

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.560
And a couple of years ago,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.360
it wasn't super common for musicians like to do home recording

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.840
or to be you know, like very knowledgeable about mixing technique.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:46.960
And nowadays almost everybody knows you know,

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.040
like at least a little bit, right?

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:54.960
So so that part became really like democratized and became really

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:55.840
accessible.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:00.000
And I think that the cool musical technology that's that some

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.200
of the neural networks offer, that's going to become like

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:06.400
democratized and accessible as well.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.240
And do you think it's because of LLM's?

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.440
Because nowadays lots of people learn programming and program

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:12.480
thanks to LLM's.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:16.720
So do you think that musicians like do it just because they feel like

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.200
they have to just to catch up, you know, like,

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:20.400
try new things?

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:21.280
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.200
And I think also it's like easier than ever to start programming, right?

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:29.040
So so if you're using like an LLM assistant, you can just,

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:31.280
you know,

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.160
take the the link to the repo or or or whatever, what you, you know,

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:39.920
don't know how to do just ask an LLM like, Hey, how do I run

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:40.800
this script?

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.640
And if you get like a trace back and like a value error or you can

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.920
ask like, Hey, what's this thing and how do I fix it?

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.760
So I think, you know, like getting into programming has gotten

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:54.480
like more accessible than ever.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:08:00.080
And it's a really good time to use that knowledge and that skill,

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.640
you know, for, you know, maybe something creative, right?

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:10.720
So musicians create music and AI models often are trained on this

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:11.280
music,

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:15.200
to publish the YouTube and Spotify, etc.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.960
Is that a problem?

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:18.560
Yeah, that's a big problem.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.960
That's a big problem.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.960
Because, you know, there's like this huge legal gap right now that's

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:27.040
being,

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.760
it's like a melting pot right now.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:31.920
So there's, you know, how does it work?

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.000
If I were a musician and I recorded a song and I published a

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.520
YouTube and everywhere, right?

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.000
How can I prevent those companies to train their models on my data?

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.240
Well, there are initiatives like in the European Union,

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.640
like the like the opt out mechanism, etc.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.560
But in practice, it's not working very well.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:58.800
So we see that if an aggressive start up wants to just

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.840
scrape your stuff from whatever you wherever you put it,

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:05.600
they just do it, right?

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:11.360
And so there is this interesting research area that's called the data poisoning

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:11.920
actually.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:23.680
So you can kind of inject like it's [...] steganography.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:26.480
That's how I should say, it's steganography.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:32.720
But in like a more kind of, you know, like deep learning modern approach,

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:33.760
right? So you

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:42.000
kind of inject some like poison pill that makes the data either

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.680
uninformative for the model.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.840
So the model doesn't to learn anything from it.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.400
Or even you can make the model kind of learn, you know, something opposite to

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:56.720
what you planned, but that technology is not well researched for audio.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:00.320
Sometimes it introduces artifacts, sometimes and it has to be

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:01.120
notable, right?

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:02.880
You don't want to break your music for it.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:05.920
Is it done by artists or is it done by those providers?

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.160
For example, YouTube Spotify, et cetera.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:12.720
Yeah, I don't think there's there's any like data poisoning that has been,

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.760
you know, put into production at scale.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:17.520
There's there's a lot of interest.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:19.280
So it's like a research area.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:24.000
It's more like a proof of concept or like a conference paper,

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:32.720
a type of, you know, level of advancement, but there's no good way of

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:34.160
defense right now, right?

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:40.160
So there's there's a bunch of legal lawsuits going going on right now in a

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:43.840
bunch of courts here and there.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:49.600
There's also no good way of reverse engineering and neural network.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:52.160
But this is also a research area.

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:56.080
There are the research areas of explainability and interpretability.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:01.040
This is also what we do here at Warsaw University of Technology and those are

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:07.360
super interesting topics, not only because of the effects, because you really

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:13.360
kind of get to, you know, kind of demystify this black box and look into its

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:18.000
internals and find, you know, like the circuits that correspond to like human

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.080
interpretable concepts.

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:22.080
But yeah, but those are still those are

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:28.640
still like research topics and not something that someone has already put into

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.080
production at scale.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:33.680
So you mentioned that AI music is a black box.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:35.760
Yeah. So can you explain how does it work?

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:41.680
So if I have, if I type in a prompt, if I type in anything, so like what

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:43.520
happens in the background, right?

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:44.560
Right.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:51.920
So what you use nowadays is, it's like almost exclusively neural

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:52.400
networks.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:56.800
So this is like the family of of AI algorithms that get that approximate

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:03.360
complex transformations of data and it's trained on data and you use GPU.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:07.040
So this is why NVIDIA is worth so much, right?

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:13.040
But what happens actually is, like, audio is a complicated modality.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:19.840
Like for a computer, audio is like this long, one-dimensional sequence of

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:20.320
numbers.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:21.520
It's like a list, right?

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.520
And it's like a super long list.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:28.880
If you have the, the, if your audio is like sample, that a standard sampling

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:36.560
rate of like 44.1 kilohertz, that means each second of audio is like 44,000

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:37.440
numbers.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:43.120
So in order to generate 10 seconds, you need like almost half a million numbers.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:49.680
The human ear is like very, it hears very little artifacts.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:55.440
It's much easier to fool the eye than to fool that ear.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:59.200
So basically when you're generating raw audio, what you're doing is your

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:04.080
modeling, like, very complex relationships between numbers that are sometimes

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:07.600
like hundreds of thousands numbers.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:10.160
So in general, it's like a very complex task.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:15.280
So basically, what we've come up with is this idea, and this is probably the

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:19.280
most scientific thing I'm going to say today is this idea of the

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:21.840
discrete representation learning.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:28.000
So basically you have a model that kind of compresses all possible sounds into

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:34.560
like this alphabet that you can then kind of generate sequences off and

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:38.240
then kind of upscale that back to audio.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:45.120
So basically like there's this learned latent alphabet that kind of, you

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.200
know, then gets upscale to audio.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:51.760
That's one of the ideas and another idea that you might have heard of is this

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:53.680
idea of diffusion.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:58.880
So there's either the, what we would call auto regressive neural codec

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:04.960
models, or there's this idea of diffusion models, which in a nutshell,

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:11.520
they take noise and they kind of distill that noise into like the precise audio

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:12.080
you want.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:19.680
So these are kind of abstract and really weird ways because many people just

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:22.880
think that neural networks just take audio and kind of cut it up like a

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:26.720
sampling machine and rearrange the samples and like call it a day, right?

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:31.280
So actually there's, there's a couple of very interesting mechanisms going on.

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:36.960
Like, you know, I'm biased in how I think about this, but, but there is some

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:43.360
like, you know, like audio understanding going on both in the, like

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:48.880
dimension of the content and like, what is there in this music is this

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:51.760
low voice or high voice or is this a guitar or a trumpet?

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:56.320
And both in terms of like the signal is the signal good quality.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:15:00.880
Is it, does it have, you know, like an in noise, like, etc.

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:04.960
So, there's a bunch of like signal understanding and content

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.320
understanding going on.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:08.960
And what are some technologies that you use for this?

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:11.240
So for example, can I train my own model?

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:12.640
Yeah, like locally.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.080
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:22.000
So there's if when people ask what's the first model that I could

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:27.680
try and play with, there's this awesome model developed at IRCAM.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:32.640
That's, that's a research facility, like a creative machine learning signal

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:35.760
processing type of institution in France.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:37.840
And there's this model called RAVE.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:42.080
It's real-time audio, variational auto encoder.

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:45.600
And it's a pretty small and very fast neural network.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:52.720
You can train it in like a day or two, like using free online resources

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:57.040
on like a small set of audio and it's a style transfer neural net.

00:15:57.040 --> 00:16:00.960
So then you can perhaps like sing into the net.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:05.200
And if you, if you trained it on, for example, like trumpet or

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:09.760
drums or something else, it's going to try to change your voice into that

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:10.240
instrument.

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:17.600
And the most fun part is when you try to do like a totally out of domain

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:24.240
style transfer, that is to kind of use an input signal that, that is as far as

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:28.480
possible from what the neural network was trained on.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:31.120
So then you get kind of these weird results.

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:38.400
And you also get this nice feature that rave models are kind of interactive and

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:39.200
steerable.

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:45.440
So there are ideas to encompass neural network within, a neural networks

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.480
within like VST plugins, which is a very musician, friendly format.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:54.800
So you get your knobs, you get your keys, you get like your control surfaces

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:56.800
you're used to.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:00.880
So yeah, I'd say RAVE, real-time audio, variational auto encoder.

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:09.440
The original author is Antoine Caillot and the GitHub repo is like open,

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:10.960
RAVE is pip installable.

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:14.160
So, you can just, I would start over there.

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:16.960
And most of those are using Python, right?

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:20.480
And absolutely most of those tools are using Python.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:26.240
I mean, there are a couple of interesting Python libraries for audio processing.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:30.000
Like, there's the classical, librosa, there is, there is pedalboard,

